      "text": "Using the identity $\\cos(x-90^\\circ)=\\sin(x)$, we can write $\\cos 150^\\circ = \\cos(90^\\circ+60^\\circ)$. Using the identity $\\cos(x+y) = \\cos(x)\\cos(y) - \\sin(x)\\sin(y)$, we can write $\\cos(90^\\circ+60^\\circ) = \\cos(90^\\circ)\\cos(60^\\circ) - \\sin(90^\\circ)\\sin(60^\\circ)$. Substituting the identities $\\cos(90^\\circ)=0$, $\\sin(90^\\circ)=1$, $\\cos(60^\\circ)=\\frac{1}{2}$, and $\\sin(60^\\circ)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we find $\\cos 150^\\circ = 0 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot 1 = \\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}$.The answer is: -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}",
