      "text": "To find \\(\\cos 150^\\circ\\), we will use the angle subtraction identity for cosine, which states:\n\\[\n\\cos(a - b) = \\cos a \\cos b + \\sin a \\sin b\n\\]\nIn this case, we have \\(a = 180^\\circ\\) and \\(b = 30^\\circ\\). Plugging these values into the identity, we get:\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = \\cos (180^\\circ - 30^\\circ)\n\\]\nUsing the difference of angles formula for cosine:\n\\[\n\\cos (180^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = \\cos 180^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ + \\sin 180^\\circ \\sin 30^\\circ\n\\]\nWe know the following values:\n\\[\n\\cos 180^\\circ = -1, \\quad \\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\quad \\sin 180^\\circ = 0, \\quad \\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\nSubstituting these into the equation:\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = (-1) \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) + (0) \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nSo, the final answer is \\(\\cos 150^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). We can use Python to verify this calculation.\n```python\nimport math\r\n\r\n# Convert the angle to radians\r\nangle_150_degrees = 150\r\nangle_150_radians = math.radians(angle_150_degrees)\r\n\r\n# Calculate cosine of 150 degrees\r\ncos_150 = math.cos(angle_150_radians)\r\nprint(cos_150)\n```\n```output\n-0.8660254037844387\n```\nThe calculated value of \\(\\cos 150^\\circ\\) using Python is approximately \\(-0.8660254037844387\\), which matches the simplified form \\(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
