      "text": "To find \\(\\cos 150^\\circ\\), we can use the angle subtraction identity for cosine. The angle \\(150^\\circ\\) can be expressed as \\(180^\\circ - 30^\\circ\\). Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = \\cos (180^\\circ - 30^\\circ)\n\\]\n\nUsing the cosine angle subtraction identity, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos (180^\\circ - \\theta) = -\\cos \\theta\n\\]\n\nIn this case, \\(\\theta = 30^\\circ\\), so:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = -\\cos 30^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know from trigonometry that \\(\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nPutting the final answer in the required format:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
